  "text": "Ava (Design): quick accessibility follow-up \u2014 VoiceOver on mac reads the topbar search twice when you tab into it. Repro: Cmd+Option+F to focus, it says 'Search, edit text' then immediately 'Search' again. Screenshot: https://figma.redwood.app/topbar-search\nMiguel (UX): hmm that's not great. I can repro on Safari. Wondering if it's the visible label + placeholder both being exposed to AT.\nPriya (Frontend): likely \u2014 we render a <label> for visual users and keep the placeholder for mobile. Maybe the DOM has both accessible. I'll inspect.\nPriya (Frontend): found it: structure is <label>Search</label><div role=\"search\"><input placeholder=\"Search\" aria-label=\"Search\" /></div>\nSam (Accessibility): yep, duplicate semantics. VoiceOver narrates label + accessible name from input + placeholder fallback. Two options: remove label from accessibility tree (aria-hidden) and keep accessible input name, or set input aria-labelledby to the label and remove aria-label/placeholder as exposed name.\nMiguel (UX): visually we need the label for aligned layout, but we don't need screen readers to hear it twice. Prefer aria-labelledby approach for correctness.\nAva (Design): agree \u2014 aria-labelledby keeps DOM order and doesn't break visuals. Also can we tweak the SR copy to include context? e.g., 'Search console' instead of plain 'Search' so it's clearer for blind users.\nSam (Accessibility): I like 'Search Redwood Console' \u2014 it's concise and gives context in lists of controls. PR should update label text used for aria-labelledby but keep the visual text as 'Search'. We can use aria-label on input with the expanded phrase and set aria-hidden on the visual label if design wants purely visual text unchanged.\nPriya (Frontend): proposed change in code:\n```\n<label id=\"topbar-search-label\">Search</label>\n<input aria-labelledby=\"topbar-search-label\" aria-label=\"Search Redwood Console\" placeholder=\"Search\" />\n```\nOr to avoid double announce just remove aria-label and change the label's text for SR only via `visually-hidden` class: `span.sr-only { content: \"Search Redwood Console\" }`\nJordan (QA): I'll add a test case: keyboard focus reads exactly 'Search Redwood Console, edit text' once. Automate with VoiceOver transcript check.\nAva (Design): also flag: focus ring contrast on the input is pretty subtle on dark theme \u2014 current ring is #3b82f6 on #0f172a; contrast ratio visually okay but some users reported hard to see. Could we increase thickness to 3px or switch to #60a5fa?\nMiguel (UX): prefer thicker ring over color change to keep color palette. 3px outline + 8px offset might help. We should verify with keyboard-only flow.\nPriya (Frontend): will add CSS tweak for :focus-visible and test both light/dark. Example:\n```\ninput:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #60a5fa; outline-offset: 2px; }\n```\nSam (Accessibility): check contrast against the background per WCAG for focus indicators (sufficient) and ensure it doesn't rely on color alone \u2014 the thickness helps.\nAva (Design): small copy nit \u2014 placeholder copy 'Search' is fine. But the SR string should be the contextualized one. Also add an aria-live announcement for search empty -> 'no results' messages? That's separate thread but flagging now.\nPriya (Frontend): I'll open a small PR implementing aria-labelledby + SR-only label change + focus ring bump. Will include screenshots and VoiceOver notes.\nJordan (QA): tagging test plan: add manual SR smoke, automated focus-only test, and visual diff for focus ring.\nSam (Accessibility): I'll review the PR and run with VoiceOver + NVDA. If we need to hide visual label from AT we can use `aria-hidden=\"true\"` on the label and keep a sr-only span for screen readers.\nAva (Design): sweet. Owners: Priya (PR), Sam (a11y review), Jordan (QA). Target: patch release this week if no major side effects. :thumbsup:\nfigma-bot: Updated Figma file: Topbar \u2014 Search (https://figma.redwood.app/file/xyz)",
